  {
    "sha": "d817529cc99dd1210fe2b03dd84818cfe2784cbd",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOV1WsOtoAKGQ4MTc1MjljYzk5ZGQxMjEwZmUyYjAzZGQ4NDgxOGNmZTI3ODRjYmQ",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Quintin Smits",
        "email": "qd.smits@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-27T12:54:29Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Quintin Smits",
        "email": "qd.smits@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-27T12:54:29Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(tools): close the three mesh-body review blockers\n\n- A spindle body grows by its pad at both axial ends of every band, so\n  an internal shoulder grows along the axis too; the outline is the\n  union of the grown bands and the safety check reads the grown bands.\n- An assembled lathe tool keeps its pad: the lathe body holds it, every\n  rebuild keeps it, and the tool body on file writes an optional strict\n  `pad` key, so save and open give the same bands.\n- A stated mounting frame, port or CIP that the STEP reader refused\n  refuses the body by name; it is never read as absent.\n\nRefs #3643\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
      "tree": {
        "sha": "5800c90dd21a7b0f47630c7628f38912526832c1",
